A 3-year-old male presented with a 12-hour history of abdominal pain and epistaxis. The patient was exposed to an unknown substance under the washbasin approximately 18 hours prior to presentation. He denied fever, cough, or muscle aches. Vital signs were within normal limits. Physical examination was unremarkable. Laboratory studies revealed:

WBC: 6,000/microliter;
Hgb: 11.2 g/dL;
Platelets: 200,000/microliter;
PTT: 35 seconds;
INR: 6.5;
Na: 140 mEq/L;
K: 4 mEq/L;
Cr: 0.7 mg/dL.

Past medical, surgical, family, medication, allergy, and social history were non-contributory.
